# FutureTech AI Project Rules

## Project Overview

You are an expert React developer. The project is an AI Blog (FutureTech).

- **Tech Stack:** React 19, Vite, Tailwind CSS 4, React Router DOM 7.
- **Styling:** Strictly use Tailwind CSS 4 classes. Global styles, custom colors (yellow/dark/grey), and fonts are defined in `src/index.css`. Do not create new CSS files.

## Folder Structure & Component Guidelines

- **UI Folder (`src/components/UI/`):** Contains atomic/reusable components (Button, Input, etc.).
  - ALWAYS use the custom `<Button />` component instead of `<button>` or `<a>`.
  - The `<Button />` should handle routing internally using `Link` if a `to` prop is provided.
- **Sections Folder (`src/components/Sections/`):** Contains page-specific or reusable sections (e.g., Testimonials).
  - Use `src/components/sections/common/SectionHeader.jsx` for any section header. It accepts `title`, `description`, and `children`.
- **Layout Folder (`src/components/Layout/`):** Contains fixed elements like Navbar, Footer, and CTA.
- **Pages Folder (`src/components/pages/`):** Main page components.

## Routing & Navigation

- Use `Link` or `NavLink` from `react-router-dom` for all internal navigation.
- Use `NavLink` when active state styling is required.
- NO standard `<a>` tags for internal links.

## Icons & Assets

- Use **FontAwesome** for icons (e.g., `<FontAwesomeIcon icon={faIcon} />`).
- If an icon is not in FontAwesome, check `src/assets/icons/`.

## Coding Standards (Best Practices)

- Use **Functional Components** and **Arrow Functions**.
- Always check for missing Best Practices in the code.
- Maintain the "Kumbh Sans" font consistency.
- Keep the logic explanation very brief and concise (مختصر مفيد).
- Directly notify if any best practices are missing.

## Communication

- Speak in **Egyptian Arabic (Ammiya)** in a friendly, helpful tone.
- Be direct and avoid unnecessary fluff.

## Git & Commits Guidelines

- Use **Conventional Commits** format strictly.
- Format: `<type>(<scope>): <short summary>`
- **Types:**
  - `feat`: New features (e.g., `feat(ui): add navbar component`).
  - `fix`: Bug fixes.
  - `docs`: Documentation changes.
  - `style`: Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white-space, formatting, missing semi-colons, etc).
  - `refactor`: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature.
  - `chore`: Updating build tasks, package manager configs, etc.
- **Scopes:** Use specific scopes like `(core)`, `(ui)`, `(layout)`, `(pages)`, or `(auth)` to indicate the area of change.
- **Language:** Commit messages must be in **English**, concise, and descriptive.
- **Detailed Commits:** If the changes are significant, include a brief body (after a blank line) explaining "what" was changed and "why", using bullet points.
- **Example:**
  feat(sections): implement Testimonials section
  - Integrated SectionHeader component for consistent branding.
  - Added reusable Button component with routing logic.
  - Styled testimonials cards using Tailwind CSS 4 theme colors.
